1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,172
The meeting has barely started and the board is full.

2
00:00:05,172 --> 00:00:06,724
Fifteen roadmap items.

3
00:00:06,724 --> 00:00:08,276
Fifteen top priorities.

4
00:00:08,276 --> 00:00:12,931
The person asking for retention has a good reason.

5
00:00:12,931 --> 00:00:16,552
So does the person asking for revenue.

6
00:00:16,552 --> 00:00:23,276
The refactor matters to the engineers who will carry the system next quarter.

7
00:00:23,276 --> 00:00:25,862
Everybody can defend their note.

8
00:00:25,862 --> 00:00:29,483
That is why the room is stuck.

9
00:00:29,483 --> 00:00:40,345
In Chapter Two, I tell a version of this planning meeting and ask what the team cannot afford to get wrong.

10
00:00:40,345 --> 00:00:44,483
That question changes the shape of the conversation.

11
00:00:44,483 --> 00:00:51,207
The book's case lands on the first minutes of a new user's experience.

12
00:00:51,207 --> 00:00:56,897
We do not need to borrow that answer for every team.

13
00:00:56,897 --> 00:01:06,724
We need to borrow the move: step away from the list and name the consequence of a wrong choice.

14
00:01:06,724 --> 00:01:10,345
Open the decision file from Lesson One.

15
00:01:10,345 --> 00:01:13,448
Read the decision at the top.

16
00:01:13,448 --> 00:01:20,172
If it still sounds like “choose all the good ideas,” make it sharper.

17
00:01:20,172 --> 00:01:24,310
Whose experience or business result is at stake?

18
00:01:24,310 --> 00:01:30,000
What would a choice teach you before the next planning cycle?

19
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,051
A long list can feel fair.

20
00:01:33,051 --> 00:01:36,102
Each person sees their concern represented.

21
00:01:36,102 --> 00:01:45,254
But the list does not tell you which problem to solve first, and it cannot tell you why.

22
00:01:45,254 --> 00:01:48,814
That is the trap in the chapter.

23
00:01:48,814 --> 00:01:56,441
More information may give every proposal a stronger defense while leaving the actual decision untouched.

24
00:01:56,441 --> 00:01:58,475
Think about airport signage.

25
00:01:58,475 --> 00:02:07,119
A terminal can offer plenty of services, yet the traveler needs to know where to go next.

26
00:02:07,119 --> 00:02:13,729
If the sign does not make that clear, more decoration will not help.

27
00:02:13,729 --> 00:02:17,797
The same thing happens in a product meeting.

28
00:02:17,797 --> 00:02:30,000
Before asking an AI tool for twenty feature ideas, ask what path the person is trying to find and what the team can change.

29
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,644
Here is a synthetic case we will use on screen: a small membership service has three requests.

30
00:02:38,644 --> 00:02:40,678
Rewrite its welcome email.

31
00:02:40,678 --> 00:02:43,220
Add a guided first task.

32
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:45,763
Build a dashboard for staff.

33
00:02:45,763 --> 00:02:47,797
They might all help.

34
00:02:47,797 --> 00:02:50,339
We do not know yet.

35
00:02:50,339 --> 00:03:00,000
Our job is to compare them against the same decision, rather than letting the loudest request become the plan.

36
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,715
Chapter Three gives us a way to structure a messy opportunity.

37
00:03:05,715 --> 00:03:11,950
The first move is to decide what question the case is answering.

38
00:03:11,950 --> 00:03:19,743
If you are asking whether a market is attractive, the book offers a market frame.

39
00:03:19,743 --> 00:03:29,095
If you are choosing a product or segment, it offers three C plus P: customer, company, competition, product.

40
00:03:29,095 --> 00:03:34,810
We are choosing a product move, so that second frame fits.

41
00:03:34,810 --> 00:03:39,486
Customer: which request responds to a real, urgent job?

42
00:03:39,486 --> 00:03:44,162
Company: what can this team actually deliver and maintain?

43
00:03:44,162 --> 00:03:52,994
Competition: how is the need handled now, even if the alternative is a spreadsheet or another person?

44
00:03:52,994 --> 00:03:58,190
Product: what is the smallest offer that could show value?

45
00:03:58,190 --> 00:04:02,346
Notice what this does to our synthetic options.

46
00:04:02,346 --> 00:04:12,218
The welcome email may be easy to ship, but we still need to know whether misunderstanding is the problem.

47
00:04:12,218 --> 00:04:18,453
The guided task might help people begin, but it could add burden.

48
00:04:18,453 --> 00:04:26,246
The staff dashboard may improve visibility for employees while leaving the member's first step untouched.

49
00:04:26,246 --> 00:04:33,000
We have turned three attractive artifacts into three questions that can be checked.

50
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,156
Now put the three options side by side.

51
00:04:37,156 --> 00:04:52,743
For each one, write the customer pain it might address, the business reason to care, the learning value of trying it, the risk, and whether you can reverse the choice.

52
00:04:52,743 --> 00:04:57,939
We do not have to invent a perfect numerical score.

53
00:04:57,939 --> 00:05:04,693
In fact, a precise score would be dishonest when the evidence is thin.

54
00:05:04,693 --> 00:05:11,966
On our sample sheet, the welcome email is low effort and easy to undo.

55
00:05:11,966 --> 00:05:17,682
Its weak point is that it assumes words are the barrier.

56
00:05:17,682 --> 00:05:24,436
The guided first task could expose whether people need a clearer starting point.

57
00:05:24,436 --> 00:05:33,268
Its weak point is that we do not know if they want guidance or a faster path.

58
00:05:33,268 --> 00:05:47,296
The staff dashboard might help the team see where members pause, but it asks for more build work before we have agreed what we need to observe.

59
00:05:47,296 --> 00:05:49,894
This is a hypothetical exercise.

60
00:05:49,894 --> 00:05:53,531
It is not a report from customers.

61
00:05:53,531 --> 00:06:01,324
You can disagree with my provisional ranking if you can name what evidence changes it.

62
00:06:01,324 --> 00:06:06,000
That is what a useful priority conversation sounds like.

63
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:18,283
There is another question that can make a promising idea fail: how does the value reach a customer, and how does the work sustain itself?

64
00:06:18,283 --> 00:06:26,636
Chapter Three brings business model options into the case after the customer and product question is clear.

65
00:06:26,636 --> 00:06:39,410
It asks whether people need to experience value before paying, whether value accumulates over time, whether a free tier is affordable, and whether usage is predictable.

66
00:06:39,410 --> 00:06:45,306
Those are decision questions, not a menu that automatically picks your business.

67
00:06:45,306 --> 00:06:51,202
Our membership example may not need a new pricing model at all.

68
00:06:51,202 --> 00:07:01,520
But if the guided task changes who gets help and who pays for it, we should say so before we build.

69
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:07,908
The book includes a B2B analytics case and names a tool called StratQuest.

70
00:07:07,908 --> 00:07:13,803
We are using neither as proof of a current product outcome here.

71
00:07:13,803 --> 00:07:22,156
What matters for this lesson is the discipline: first structure the case, then consider the economic fit.

72
00:07:22,156 --> 00:07:25,104
An AI brainstorm can offer possibilities.

73
00:07:25,104 --> 00:07:31,000
It cannot supply the missing customer and operating evidence by sounding certain.

74
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:38,787
For the sample case, I would choose a small test of the guided first task.

75
00:07:38,787 --> 00:07:43,978
I would not commit to a full guided onboarding product.

76
00:07:43,978 --> 00:07:56,437
I would show a simple first step to a few consenting participants through an approved research process, then ask whether it helped them begin.

77
00:07:56,437 --> 00:08:05,262
I would keep the welcome email as an alternative if the test shows the language is confusing.

78
00:08:05,262 --> 00:08:14,607
I would hold the staff dashboard until the team knows which question it needs the dashboard to answer.

79
00:08:14,607 --> 00:08:19,279
My reason is not that guided tasks always win.

80
00:08:19,279 --> 00:08:27,585
It is that this option can test the starting-point assumption while keeping the next commitment small.

81
00:08:27,585 --> 00:08:30,699
Your decision may go another way.

82
00:08:30,699 --> 00:08:46,273
Put your choice at the top of the page and write the sentence that makes it defensible: “We choose this because...” Then record the two alternatives you are setting aside.

83
00:08:46,273 --> 00:08:58,213
Give each one a real reason, not a vague “lower priority.” A decision file that only celebrates the winner loses half the learning.

84
00:08:58,213 --> 00:09:06,000
When new evidence arrives, those alternatives tell you what you thought you were trading away.

85
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:08,656
Pause here and finish the page.

86
00:09:08,656 --> 00:09:17,066
You need the chosen option, two alternatives, the reason for each, and one assumption that could overturn your choice.

87
00:09:17,066 --> 00:09:23,705
If you are working with a real team, keep sensitive inputs in the approved place.

88
00:09:23,705 --> 00:09:29,902
You can do this exercise with a fictional case and still practice the decision.

89
00:09:29,902 --> 00:09:33,885
When you come back, read your assumption out loud.

90
00:09:33,885 --> 00:09:37,869
Is it something you know, or something you hope?

91
00:09:37,869 --> 00:09:42,738
In the next lesson we will work backward from that sentence.

92
00:09:42,738 --> 00:09:49,820
We will ask what answer the decision owner needs and what evidence would change the conversation.

93
00:09:49,820 --> 00:09:53,803
The board of fifteen priorities will still exist tomorrow.

94
00:09:53,803 --> 00:10:00,000
You have done the harder thing today: made one choice clear enough to challenge.
